<p>Today's brief covers stories from 19 to 20 November.</p>

<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Edge</b><span>A remote support appliance is compromised through a zero-day</span></li>
<li><b>Foothold</b><span>Attackers move to an internal appliance and gain root</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Control plane</b><span>The vCenter authentication bypass gives access to virtualisation</span></li>
<li><b>Persist</b><span>Malware hijacks tokens and creates administrator accounts</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Exfiltrate</b><span>Databases and virtual machine data are taken</span></li>
<li><b>Fix</b><span>Patches arrive more than two months after exploitation began</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>The vCenter campaign as described by Google's researchers: edge tool first, control plane second.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. The European Commission confirms an AI-assisted cyberattack</h2>
<p>The European Commission confirmed that an AI-assisted cyberattack, detected on 22 October, disrupted staff services including its HR platform Sysper, the MyPMO medical reimbursement system and translation services, with some outages lasting more than ten days<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The Commission says it has found no evidence of a data breach so far and that the investigation continues; press reports link the campaign to a publicly available AI penetration testing framework, which the Commission has not confirmed<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> the practical change from AI-assisted attacks is speed and persistence, not new techniques. Attackers can try more paths, faster, against everything you expose. That makes the basics more urgent: phishing-resistant sign-in, quick patching of internet-facing systems, segmentation so one compromised service does not reach the rest, and detection tuned to spot rapid, automated reconnaissance. European public bodies and their suppliers should expect questions on exactly these controls. See the <a href="/case-studies/uk-payments-firm-ai-cyber-risk-controls/">AI cyber risk controls case study</a> and my <a href="/fractional-cto-europe/">Europe page</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. The vCenter zero-day campaign began with a BeyondTrust flaw</h2>
<p>Google's Mandiant and Threat Intelligence Group attribute exploitation of the vCenter authentication bypass to Armory Castle, a suspected China-nexus espionage group, which first compromised BeyondTrust Remote Support instances through a separate zero-day in late August, then moved to an internal appliance and on to vCenter from early September<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Google says a small number of organisations are confirmed compromised and others are likely affected<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> this is the pattern from this month's <a href="/insights/vendor-admin-access-rmm-breaches/">vendor admin access analysis</a> playing out: a remote access tool at the edge, then the virtualisation control plane. If you run BeyondTrust Remote Support or vCenter, patch both, then hunt rather than assume: look for new administrator and single sign-on accounts, unusual vCenter logins and unexpected outbound traffic from management appliances since late August. Keep management interfaces on networks that remote support tools cannot reach directly.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. Transport for London discloses an Oyster photocard breach</h2>
<p>Transport for London said about 265,000 Oyster photocard customers were affected after it detected suspicious activity on 16 November and took the photocard system offline; reported exposed data includes names, contact details, addresses and security questions and answers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. TfL says passwords and payment card details were not compromised, and customers will be asked to reset passwords and security answers when the portal returns<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> security questions are the weak point here. Once their answers leak, they are useful to attackers on every other service that uses the same questions, and customers cannot change their mother's maiden name. If your product still uses knowledge-based questions for recovery, replace them with stronger recovery factors. UK organisations should also note how quickly the regulator was informed. See the <a href="/case-studies/uk-saas-sso-staff-access-hardening/">access hardening case study</a> and my <a href="/fractional-cto-united-kingdom/">UK page</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. A filing names Anthropic as tenant of a $28 billion Texas lease</h2>
<p>Riot Platforms had disclosed a 15-year lease of its Corsicana, Texas campus to a frontier AI lab, with total contract value of about $28 billion<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>. Its latest quarterly filing names Anthropic as the tenant<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> long leases like this are how AI providers secure future capacity, and they are a useful signal for customers. A provider that has locked in power and space is better placed to honour capacity commitments, but also carries fixed costs that shape its pricing. When negotiating, ask providers about capacity guarantees and regions, not just per-token prices. See <a href="/insights/microsoft-oracle-gpu-capacity-contracts/">AI capacity contracts</a> and the <a href="/case-studies/us-saas-multi-region-ai-capacity/">multi-region AI capacity case study</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Patch BeyondTrust Remote Support and vCenter, then hunt for new admin and SSO accounts since late August.</li>
<li>Check that management interfaces are unreachable from remote support and user networks.</li>
<li>Replace security questions in account recovery with stronger factors.</li>
<li>Review detection for fast, automated reconnaissance against internet-facing services.</li>
<li>Ask AI providers about capacity guarantees in your regions at the next renewal.</li>
</ul>
<p>I am a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> and <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architect</a> based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. If any of these lands on your desk this week, <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
